<p>President Donald Trump said the United States is preparing a “third wave” of military strikes against Iran, as the focus shifts from targeting decisions to the harder question of who could lead Iran after the war. Trump told reporters that many Iranians the U.S. had viewed as possible postwar leaders were killed in the strikes, leaving the succession picture deeply uncertain. Trump said he chose to go to war to head off what he described as an imminent Iranian attack, while warning that the “worst case” would be Iran ending up with a replacement “as bad” as Khamenei. He also suggested that a leader “from within” Iran’s existing structure might be the best outcome after the war, signaling caution about trying to engineer a sweeping political overhaul.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/Ol5wSv4IeKrD3du3aS5KKGczdk43uAzKK7NQe2EjLMEY4WGMfegZ44FB0hT41-rP0Db_QFxbbQTfOJafebOKtHhGfUW2QrgL9QvClf_Jo1N9ZD217EyXN5ek74omg7uFjbqrMqrWfcduRP8HwDJKu-ldt0xouFU7dcOjJWhVOvgdO4sFaPYXqypwe9A3_xxzIl2rUHL2XJPXjegtGHKJNOuLyOHfFFto' alt='Tehran after airstrikes on Tuesday. President Trump said he chose war to pre-empt Iran, which he claimed was about to attack.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Venezuela analogy: Trump described one outcome he wants to avoid by sketching a postwar Iran that resembles Venezuela’s political system.</li><li>Reza Pahlavi floated (but not embraced): Trump raised exiled Iranian dissident Reza Pahlavi while discussing succession. Reporting said he seemed to dismiss him as the answer even as he weighed other possibilities.</li><li>Operation context: Trump’s “third wave” warning followed what NDTV described as the early phases of “Operation Epic Fury,” which it said included the elimination of Supreme Leader Ayatollah Ali Khamenei.</li></ul><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>President Donald Trump: He said Iranians the U.S. had viewed as potential postwar leaders were killed in the strikes, and warned the “worst case” would be a successor “as bad” as Khamenei. (<